.Dd May 28, 2026
.Dt XTC_SUPERVISOR 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm xtc_sup_start ,
.Nm xtc_sup_add_child ,
.Nm xtc_sup_stop ,
.Nm xtc_sup_join ,
.Nm xtc_sup_alive ,
.Nm xtc_sup_n_children ,
.Nm xtc_sup_n_alive ,
.Nm xtc_sup_n_restarts
.Nd OTP-style supervisor trees
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In xtc.h
.In xtc_orc.h
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_sup_start "xtc_loop_t *loop" "const xtc_sup_opts_t *opts" "const xtc_child_spec_t *children" "int n_children" "xtc_supervisor_t **out_sup"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_sup_add_child "xtc_supervisor_t *sup" "const xtc_child_spec_t *spec" "xtc_pid_t *out_pid"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_sup_stop "xtc_supervisor_t *sup"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_sup_join "xtc_supervisor_t *sup" "int64_t timeout_ns"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_sup_alive "const xtc_supervisor_t *sup"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_sup_n_children "const xtc_supervisor_t *sup"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_sup_n_alive "const xtc_supervisor_t *sup"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_sup_n_restarts "const xtc_supervisor_t *sup"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm xtc_supervisor
is an OTP-style supervisor: it owns a set of child specs, spawns
them, observes failures, and restarts them per the configured
strategy.  Failures isolate to children; the supervisor's own
liveness is independent of any single child.
.Pp
.Sy Strategies
control which children get restarted on a child crash:
.Bl -tag -width "simple_one_for_one" -offset indent
.It Dv XTC_SUP_ONE_FOR_ONE
Only the crashed child restarts.
.It Dv XTC_SUP_ONE_FOR_ALL
All children restart.
.It Dv XTC_SUP_REST_FOR_ONE
The crashed child and all started after it
.Pq right siblings
restart, in order.
.It Dv XTC_SUP_SIMPLE_ONE_FOR_ONE
Children spawned dynamically; not all known up front.
.El
.Pp
.Sy Group restarts never overlap.
Under
.Dv XTC_SUP_ONE_FOR_ALL
and
.Dv XTC_SUP_REST_FOR_ONE
the supervisor asks every affected child to exit and then WAITS until
each one's cleanup has completed -- until it has left the process table,
which happens only after all of its
.Xr xtc_proc_at_exit 3
hooks have returned -- before it starts any replacement.
A replacement therefore never runs beside a predecessor that still holds
a shared resource.
.Pp
The wait is bounded, at 5 seconds.
If an old child has not finished cleaning up by then, the supervisor does
NOT start a replacement beside it; it stops instead, which its caller
observes through
.Fn xtc_sup_join .
.Pp
Releases before 1.50 killed the siblings, marked them dead and respawned
at once, so a replacement could run while its predecessor was still
inside an exit hook.
.Pp
.Sy Restart policies
per child:
.Bl -tag -width "XTC_RESTART_TRANSIENT" -offset indent
.It Dv XTC_RESTART_PERMANENT
Always restart on exit
.Pq normal or crash .
.It Dv XTC_RESTART_TRANSIENT
Restart on abnormal exit only.
.It Dv XTC_RESTART_TEMPORARY
Never restart; one-shot.
.El
.Pp
.Sy Sized structs:
.Vt xtc_sup_opts_t
.Pq Dv XTC_SUP_OPTS_DEFAULT
and every
.Vt xtc_child_spec_t
.Pq Dv XTC_CHILD_SPEC_INIT
start with a
.Va size
member set to the caller's
.Fn sizeof .
The library reads only that many bytes (later fields default), strides the
.Fa children
array by
.Va children[0].size ,
and returns
.Dv XTC_E_INVAL
for a
.Va size
of 0 or elements of different sizes.
.Pp
.Sy Restart intensity:
.Va opts.max_restarts
restarts within
.Va opts.period_ns
exhausts the supervisor; it gives up and exits.  Default:
1 in 5 seconds.
.Pp
.Sy Bounded dynamic pool:
for the
.Dv XTC_SUP_SIMPLE_OFO
strategy, set
.Va opts.max_children No > 0
to cap the number of live dynamic children;
.Fn xtc_sup_add_child
returns
.Dv XTC_E_RESOURCE
once the cap is reached (a slot freed by a child's DOWN is reusable).
.Va opts.max_children No == 0
is unbounded.
.Fn xtc_sup_n_children
returns the number of children the supervisor manages: every static
child (from
.Fn xtc_sup_start ,
alive or not) plus every dynamic child that is alive or being
restarted.  A dynamic child that exits for good
.Pq Dv XTC_RESTART_TEMPORARY , No or Dv XTC_RESTART_TRANSIENT No with a normal exit
is removed from the supervisor and no longer counted (before 1.55.1 its
slot was kept forever);
.Fn xtc_sup_n_alive
returns only the currently-live children (pool utilization).
.Pp
.Fn xtc_sup_start
spawns the supervisor and its initial children.  Returns
.Dv XTC_OK
with
.Fa *out_sup
set, or
.Dv XTC_E_INVAL
on bad args
.Pq a negative Fa n_children , No or Fa children No NULL with Fa n_children No > 0 .
A supervisor with no initial children
.Pq Fa n_children No == 0
is supported: children are added later with
.Fn xtc_sup_add_child .
.Pp
.Fn xtc_sup_add_child
adds one child to a running supervisor from a
.Vt xtc_child_spec_t ,
returning the new child's pid in
.Fa *out_pid .
This is the dynamic path used with a
.Sy simple_one_for_one
supervisor, whose children are spawned on demand rather than all
listed up front.
It must be called from a process; it waits up to 5 seconds for the
supervisor's reply
.Pq Er XTC_E_AGAIN No if none comes ,
and takes only that reply from the caller's mailbox: other messages,
monitor DOWNs included, stay queued.
Before 2.1.2 it read and freed every message that arrived while it
waited.
.Pp
From the supervisor's own fiber (in particular from an
.Va on_child_down
hook)
.Fn xtc_sup_add_child
returns
.Er XTC_E_STATE
at once: the supervisor cannot answer a request while it is the one
waiting for the answer.
.Ss Observing child exits
Set
.Va opts.on_child_down
.Pq and optionally Va opts.on_child_down_user ,
added in 2.2,
to be told about every child exit:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
typedef void (*xtc_sup_down_fn)(xtc_supervisor_t *sup,
    const xtc_child_spec_t *spec, const xtc_down_info_t *info,
    void *user);
.Ed
.Pp
The hook runs on the supervisor's fiber, once per DOWN of a child the
supervisor owns, and
.Em before
the supervisor decides whether to restart that child.
It is called for a child that crashed, exited or was killed by someone
else; for each sibling a
.Dv XTC_SUP_ONE_FOR_ALL
or
.Dv XTC_SUP_REST_FOR_ONE
restart kills, before that sibling is respawned; and for each child a
stop kills, before the stop completes.
.Fa info
is the classified DOWN
.Pq see Fn xtc_down_decode_ex No in Xr xtc_proc 3 :
.Va info->kind
tells a crash
.Pq Dv XTC_DOWN_KIND_SIGNAL , No signal in Va info->signal
from an exit
.Pq Dv XTC_DOWN_KIND_EXIT , No code in Va info->exit_code
from a clean return.
.Fa spec
is the supervisor's copy of the child's spec;
.Va spec->name
and
.Va spec->arg
identify the child across restarts, which its pid does not.
Both pointers are borrowed for the call.
.Pp
The hook observes; it cannot veto or alter the restart.
It must be brief and must not park
.Po no
.Fn xtc_recv ,
.Fn xtc_proc_sleep ,
blocking I/O or
.Vt xtc_amutex
.Pc :
recording the event, an atomic store,
.Fn xtc_proc_wake
and
.Fn xtc_sup_stop
are fine.
Do not forward the DOWN with a plain
.Fn xtc_send :
it honours the receiver's mailbox cap and can fail.
When the supervisor process itself is killed or faults, the children it
kills on the way out are not reported: its mailbox is already closed.
A caller built against an older
.Vt xtc_sup_opts_t
passes a smaller
.Va size ,
and gets no hook.
.Pp
A
.Dv XTC_RESTART_TEMPORARY
child with a hook is the way to own a child the embedder must never see
restarted but must see die: for example, escalating a crash
.Pq Dv XTC_DOWN_KIND_SIGNAL
to a process-wide fail-stop while letting a clean exit pass.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_sup_stop
asynchronously requests shutdown.  Children get
.Sq EXIT
signals; the supervisor exits when all are done.
.Fn xtc_sup_join
waits up to
.Fa timeout_ns
for the supervisor to exit, then frees it
.Pq \-1 waits forever, 0 polls once .
It returns
.Dv XTC_OK
once the supervisor has exited and been freed; the pointer is then
invalid.
If the supervisor is still running when the wait ends it returns
.Er XTC_E_AGAIN
and frees nothing: the pointer stays valid, so the caller can
.Fn xtc_sup_stop
it and join again.
Call it from outside the supervisor's loop thread.
Releases before 1.50 ignored the wait's result and freed the supervisor
anyway, so a join that timed out returned
.Dv XTC_OK
and freed memory the still-running supervisor was using.
.Pp
The shutdown \(em kill every child, wait for its cleanup, then report
the exit to
.Fn xtc_sup_join
and
.Fn xtc_sup_alive
\(em runs on every exit of the supervisor process: a stop, a
restart-intensity give-up, an
.Xr xtc_exit_pid 3
of the supervisor itself, or a contained fault in it.
Releases before 2.1.2 ran it only on the first two, so a killed
supervisor left its children running unsupervised and
.Fn xtc_sup_join
returned
.Er XTC_E_AGAIN
forever.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr xtc_app 3 ,
.Xr xtc_proc 3 ,
.Xr xtc_svr 3 ,
.Xr xtc 7
.Sh HISTORY
